A||||8||Fierochic88||Cleaning Diamond Spoke Rims - Need Help||||1||
Z||000000||Fierochic88||11-27-2001||08:21 PM||||Hello all -<BR>   It seems like many of us out there have those grey 15" diamond spoke rims.  I have a set of a car right now that are pretty filthy.  I need to get them really clean and am looking to see if there is a better way to do it.  Currently TooManyFieros and I are using a special drill bit and a tire brush with about the same success, the drill bit is easier to me.  The problem is finding a good cleaning product to get the interior of the spokes really clean.  We have tried PrepSol, GoofOff, Mother's Aluminum/Mag Polish, Buffing Compound and one or two other products.  The Mag Polish and Prep Sol work the best, but still not too great.  Does anyone have any suggestions to shine up those rims without totally breaking our backs?  Thanks much!<P>Jennifer <P>------------------<BR>IM Username:  FieroChic88<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<BR>Cobalt Blue 85 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.personal.psu.edu/users/j/j/jjr161/Fierochic88Signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||XDrewX||11-27-2001||08:34 PM||||I will tell you how I do it.<BR>Probably the hard way but they look Mint when done.<P>I use Mequires Tough Stain spray, I might not have the name right, I know it is Mequires and it is purple.<P>Soak the rim in that, take a Reach toothbrush, It has extended bristles on the end that just fit in the little "Diamonds", <BR>And scrub the crevises, the Mequires will take the rest off.  Spray off and wipe dry.<P>Dont use eagle one for wheels it says safe foe clear coated wheels, but my buddy found out it is not, He now has frosted wheels.<P>Peace, <P>Andrew<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||11-27-2001||08:49 PM||||Jennifer, At the FOCOA nationals, they had sample spray bottles of "Greased Lightning" high performance cleaner and degreaser.  Purple lable.<P>I started using it for car cleaning and this stuff is strong..wear rubber gloves.  Spray on the lace wheels let sit, scrub with a tooth brush, rinse, rinse and rinse. Do one wheel at a time and don't let it dry. Don't let it sit too long on the bare metal.  It will clean that up. Sort of etches it (dulls the finish), so you neeed a metal polish to get that back to life.  I used it to get the bugs off the front, and they just melted off.  It also takes the wax off, don't let it set on unprotected painted surfaces.  Rinse, rinse.<P>I found it at Kmart $4.97 a gallon.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Banzai||11-28-2001||06:34 AM||||Jennifer, try using Simple Green Automotive. I've used "purple" cleaners for years and they are too harsh for some jobs. I've found that the Simple green is just as strong but it won't harm finishes. Wet surface, spray cleaner, brush, brush, brush and rinse well.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||revin||11-28-2001||07:31 AM||||I like the drill thing ! smart girl! Now I just need some kind of brush attachment! I have seen some pointed bristled brushes that seem to get into the small cracks. I still like the drill thing!I have used some purple bottle from wal-mart. a degreaser and it seems to work real good on the "hard" stuff.I do have the black diamond spokes and WISH I had the black off them so the silver would show!! any ideas on that?<P>------------------<BR>Revin||||reg||1
Z||000005||KissMySSFiero||11-28-2001||08:21 AM||||I use simple green.  works great!<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>88FORMULA<BR>       <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>        <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||11-28-2001||09:18 AM||||I had great results on my set of Tech rims with a Black and Decker Mouse sander. Works on the surfaces really well. For the inside areas I used a Dremel with a wire brush attachment.<P>I refinished a set of four wheels this way in about 2 1/2 hours and they still look great.||||reg||1
Z||000007||California Kid||11-28-2001||09:32 AM||||No one mentioned this one, Eagle Products makes Mag Wheel Cleaners that work pretty well in my opinion. The make one for painted alloy wheels which I've used on my '87 with very good results. A lot depends on what condition the paint on the wheels is in, if the paint is pitted (by harsh chemicals) they are going to be hard to clean no matter what you use. In this case you'd be better off to repaint and/or have them powder coated them.||||reg||1
Z||000008||camon||11-28-2001||12:54 PM||||I use simple green as well with pretty good results!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||14
